 win if White defends correctly.\n    *   **Horizontal H7-I7:** Black has a \'Live 2\' here. If Black plays at J7, they would form a \'Live 3\' (Open 3) of H7-I7-J7, gaining a strong attack.\n3.  **Candidate Evaluation:**\n    *   **J7:** This is the most critical "Vital Point" on the board.\n        *   **Offense:** It extends White\'s diagonal line to form a Live 3 (H9-I8-J7), threatening to form a Live 4 on the next turn.\n        *   **Defense:** It physically blocks Black from extending their horizontal line (H7-I7) to J7, denying Black their own Live 3.\n    *   **F10:** White could block Black\'s potential \'Four\'. However, this is a purely defensive move. Black would likely respond by playing J7 themselves, taking the initiative with an Open 3.\n    *   **G10:** This extends White\'s line to G10-H9-I8, but allows Black to play J7, which blocks White\'s other side and creates a threat for Black.\n\n**Conclusion:**\nPlaying at **J7** is the optimal move. Although Black can temporarily play F10 to force a response from White, Black will run out of immediate checks and will inevitably have to turn back to defend against White\'s Open 3 generated by the move at J7. Thus, J7 secures the initiative and prevents Black from developing a strong attack.\n\nEvaluation: The move at J7 creates a simultaneous attack (Open 3) and defense (blocks opponent\'s Open 3), securing the initiative.\nReason: J7 extends the H9-I8 diagonal to form an Open 3 for White, forcing Black to defend. It also critically blocks Black from playing J7 to form their own Open 3 from H7-I7. This double-purpose move controls the flow of the game.\nAnswer: {"col": "J", "row": 7}'}
